===Etymology===
Borrowed in the Middle Ages from a {{der|en|gml|-}} variant of [[Theodoric]], and revived in the nineteenth century.<ref>Patrick Hanks and Flavia Hodges: A Concise Dictionary of First Names.Oxford University Press 2001.</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdɛɹɪk/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛɹɪk|s=2}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{given name|en|male|from=Germanic languages}}.
#* '''1895''', ''The American Magazine'', Crowell-Collier Pub. co.,1895. page 446:
#*: "Do you think so? Her husband has an odd name - '''Derek''' Keppel. He is a musician - a violinist."
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1974|author=w:Joseph Heller|title=Something happened|isbn=0224010654|page=509
|passage=We do not entertain as much anymore because of '''Derek'''. (He produces strain. We have to pretend he doesn't.) I used to like him when I still thought he was normal. I was fond of him. I used to call him Dirk, and Kiddo, Steamshovel, Dinky Boy, and Dicky Dare. Till I found out what he was. Now it's always formal: '''Derek'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=[[w:James Robertson (novelist)|James Robertson]]|title=And the Land Lay Still|publisher=Hamish Hamilton|isbn=9780241143568|page=292
|passage=Frederick or '''Derek''' Boothby was in his late fifties, the son of a naval officer and a daughter of the Earl of Limerick.}}
# {{surname|en}}.

